---
[`zml.Platform`](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/zml/platform.zig)
holds the backend runtime and devices. The initializer options are
`zml.platform.CreateOptions`. Pass a `std.mem.Allocator` for `allocator`
and a `std.Io` for `io`. A return type with `!` can carry an error.

# [Initialize a platform]($heading.id('platform'))

- `zml.Platform.auto(allocator, io, options)`\
  `!*Platform`. Try backends in priority order and return the first one that initializes. Return `error.Unavailable` if none initialize.
- `zml.Platform.init(allocator, io, target, options)`\
  `!*Platform`. Initialize a specific `zml.Target`, such as `.cpu` or `.cuda`, when present in the build.

See [Build and run with Bazel]($link.page('zml/developer-guide/build-and-run-with-bazel')) for backend flags
and the selection order used by `auto`.

# [Compile a model or function]($heading.id('compile-a-model-or-function'))

Both compile calls take `zml.Compiler.Options` and return a loaded
[Exe]($link.page('zml/api-reference/exe')).

- `platform.compile(allocator, io, model, .forward, .{input}, options)`\
  `!Exe`. Trace the named method of `model`. Pass the remaining method arguments in a tuple, as with `input` here.
- `platform.compileFn(allocator, io, function, args, options)`\
  `!Exe`. Trace a function with the full tuple of its arguments.

# [Release the platform]($heading.id('release-the-platform'))

- `platform.deinit(allocator, io)`\
  `void`. Release the platform after its buffers and executables have been released.
